3 #edb/ref 17592186120677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120046 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120046 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120046 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120046 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120046 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Span of Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120046 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120046 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120046 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120046 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "045805f2-d7d9-501d-8e4b-541e2bb65e50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120303 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120303 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120303 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120303 #edb/ref 1071 "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120303 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d12a13d-137e-5159-9c9a-8fb99c09543c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120303 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=-6 \\\\ -7{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=-11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -7 & -5 & -11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ -7 & -5 & -11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-9{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+(-2){a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot (-2)=-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120304 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120304 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34207" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120304 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π: r = sb + tc$, $t, s ∈ R$, where $b = ⟨1, 5, 8⟩$, $c = ⟨ - 1,-1,-3⟩$, and $u = ⟨ - 3, 5,-7⟩$. In the affirmative case, find the value of $s \\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u = sb + tc$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120304 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc1044df-5089-5bf6-ad79-aebf9c199eb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120676 #edb/ref 1061 17469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05c77385-415b-5833-b935-9be3a2038c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1061 17471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74f8be2d-0f86-52de-8277-fd7db395c80d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1061 17477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15713f7b-cd11-55c3-93fd-8040ac62d36f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120679 #edb/ref 1061 17478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120679 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33abdb88-235d-550f-b5bb-06946454437c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120679 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1061 23264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5cca50af-2a48-5696-bc3a-fc57e95232b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1064 "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1065 3193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c4e2e270-f1fb-5633-9239-4696e8f0a25b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1064 "The linear span of vectors can be interpreted geometrically as the points in space that can be reached by adding multiples of those vectors.\n\n- The span of a single nonzero vector is a *line* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ determines the *line* with direction vector $\\mathbf{a}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/dd/ddef437718d3a11811da02a17e2207d75e2d1852eeada67ab494af45b2a655e2.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ represents the line $l$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/08/080aa6203494e915e28c491a3d86176f7ba634bcbdfe40b094c07d85fb29bb69.png)\n\n- The span of two non-parallel vectors is a *plane* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\not\\parallel \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel), then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$ determines the *plane* with direction vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/1f/1f92378ff1d1409f0928caa4b3ce98861784d0f2aa51dd1eda30e8877a0134ff.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$  represents the plane $\\Pi$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/56/5606535bd91eb35745a9852ceb451720339105f5a97fb66ccfcb1164ecd55ffd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1065 3194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c50c93be-7bf2-5511-b456-c60478a53270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1067 "Geometric Interpretation of the Linear Span" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238221 #edb/ref 1080 "36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238221 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238221 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "145063fc-8e8e-5c5e-b9c0-4ce50643aeca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238333 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238333 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238333 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "202fca4b-d4db-559c-86f9-155db3d13c64" #ed